<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walwal incident</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wal Wal incident</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Walwal incident was a clash on 5 December 1934 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> of Walwal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>, between Ethiopian troops and the Italian colonial garrison, which had occupied the oasis inside Ethiopian territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> demanded compensation and an apology, and the dispute, taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, became the Abyssinia Crisis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> used it as a pretext for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in October 1935.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walwal-incident.jpg" alt="Walwal incident" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passo_Uarieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
